     1	# Hornhoof Edge
     2	
     3	The relay behind the public links of [Hornhoof](https://github.com/Invariant0x/hornhoof) companies.
     4	
     5	## What it does
     6	
     7	It gives each company a link like `https://acme.hornhoof.org`. The company's own machine opens a tunnel to the relay, so nothing needs to open on the company's router. A visitor opens the link, and the relay passes the request through that company's tunnel to its Hornhoof app.
     8	
     9	The relay hands out names, checks each tunnel's key, and sends each request to the company named in its address.
    10	
    11	A company on several machines gives each one its own client id, so two companies can run side by side on one machine and no two machines log in as one. A company's machines also reach each other's services through the relay: a `-peer` machine offers two stcp services, `-data` and `-app`, and another machine of the same link visits them with the link's visitor key and the service's own secret. The relay lets those machines reach each other through it and passes their bytes on as they come; [Hornhoof's security page](https://github.com/Invariant0x/hornhoof/blob/main/SECURITY.md#the-hornhooforg-link) says how the machines encrypt them.
    12	
    13	## What it sees
    14	
    15	At present, the browser's https connection ends at hornhoof.org. The relay reads each request to find its company, then passes it through that company's tunnel. This includes the contents of requests and answers. It keeps no copy of what passes.
    16	
    17	This is the same behavior described in [Hornhoof's security page](https://github.com/Invariant0x/hornhoof/blob/main/SECURITY.md#the-hornhooforg-link).
    18	
    19	## What it never does
    20	
    21	- Run a company's code.
    22	- Hold a company's data, files or passwords in storage.
    23	- Keep accounts.
    24	- Reach through a tunnel to anything but the Hornhoof app.
    25	- Keep a copy of the requests and answers it passes.
    26	
    27	## What it stores
    28	
    29	For each link, the relay keeps:
    30	
    31	- its name and a hash of its key;
    32	- when and from which address it was made;
    33	- when its tunnel was last seen;
    34	- whether it is blocked, when it was blocked, and why;
    35	- which client holds its route, with that client's run id, since when, and in which registration (a counter that grows by one at every registration); the route's state (pending, held, empty) and type (http, https). The relay reads whether a route is live from frps itself, so it never answers "empty" for a name it is not sure of;
    36	- its sync record: on or off, the copy's machine, its epoch, the registration it came in and when. Only the live route's holder can set a newer epoch. The relay commits it to SQLite with full synchronization before answering, and keeps it across restarts;
    37	- a hash of its visitor key, the key a visitor-only client logs in with. The key itself is handed out once and never stored or logged.
    38	
    39	For each address, it also keeps the times of new links made in the last hour, to limit how many it can make. The relay's database is SQLite.
    40	
    41	The relay logs each link made or freed with the address it came from. frps logs each tunnel's login. On the server, both logs go to the system journal.
    42	
    43	## Run the tests
    44	
    45	You need Python 3.12 or newer, Git, curl and tar, on macOS or Linux. The tests use only Python's standard library. The first run downloads frps and frpc 0.71.1 from their release and checks the archive's SHA-256 before using it.
    46	
    47	From this folder:
    48	
    49	```sh
    50	sh relay/test/frp.sh
    51	python3 -m unittest discover -s relay/test -v
    52	```
    53	
    54	The tests start their own relay, frps, frpc and small web server on local ports, then stop them. They never connect to the public relay. The publication checks read tracked files and files Git would add, respecting `.gitignore`. If `EDGE_PRIVATE_WORDS` names a file, or `~/.hornhoof-edge/private-words` exists, they also check that none of its private values appears in those files.
    55	
    56	## What is where
    57	
    58	- `relay/relay.py`: the name API, tunnel checks and operator commands, in one Python file.
    59	- `relay/frps.toml`: frps settings; the server password is a placeholder.
    60	- `relay/setup-relay.sh`, `relay/hornhoof-relay` and the service files: server setup and operator commands.
    61	- `Caddyfile`: the web server settings, the API and tunnel entry points, and company traffic.
    62	- `pages/`: the page template, build script and fonts. `pages/build.py` builds `www/index.html` and the relay's 404 and 410 pages.
    63	- `relay/test/`: tests and their local server harness. Downloaded binaries stay in the ignored `.bin/` folder.
    64	- `deploy.env.example`: example deployment settings. Real settings, access keys and other private files stay outside this repository.
    65	
    66	## License
    67	
    68	The code is under the [MIT License](LICENSE). The Onest and Tektur fonts are under [OFL-1.1](pages/fonts/OFL.txt).

